    "created_at": "2026-09-18T03:42:45Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-09-18T04:02:07Z",
    "body": "@trusktr \n\n> A connectedParsedCallback would be nifty to avoid work until parsing is done, but we probably also need a way to know when we get connected without parsing.\n\nIt sounds like the specific problem as you described it can already be resolved with a straightforward check to see if `document.readyState` is still `loading` in an existing `connectedCallback` and, if so, waiting to do your initialization by listening to the `DOMContentLoaded` event. Similar situation for @Jamesernator's problem in https://github.com/whatwg/dom/issues/662#issuecomment-404054274. In his case, the issue isn't with the `.define()` timing per se, it's just that the parser is still working. \n\n<details><summary>Essentially this...</summary>\n\nModified from example at https://github.com/whatwg/dom/issues/662#issuecomment-404054274:\n\n```js\nclass XModuleTest extends HTMLScriptElement {\n\tconnectedCallback() {\n\t\tif (document.readyState === 'loading') {\n\t\t\t// Still parsing children, wait for DOMContentLoaded or readystatechange...\n\t\t\tdocument.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => this.init())\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t// HTML children parsed into DOM already\n\t\t\tthis.init()\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tinit() {\n\t\tconsole.log(this.text)\n\t}\n}\n```\n</details>\n\nHowever, that's watching global state and we want something faster than that.\n\nSpecifically: I think this GitHub issue is more concerned about explicitly letting us know when the HTML parser itself is done parsing a particular element's HTML contents and has moved on in the DOM tree, effectively reaching the closing tag of that element while still in the `loading` state (i.e. still parsing). This is tricky you realize that other scripts on the page (including the current element) can _also_ mutate both children and adjacent siblings while the parser is also actively parsing and updating the DOM. I believe the goal here would be to have a known _consistent and deterministic_ way in which the parser can signal to an element that it has completed processing that particular element's HTML contents regardless of that. \n\nThat's why @franktopel's polyfill for this at https://github.com/WebReflection/html-parsed-element is so interesting to me, because it acts much more quickly without relying on global state. It relies on a `MutationObserver` that basically waits until it sees a `.nextSibling` and fires a custom `parsedCallback` in a `requestAnimationFrame`. This isn't exactly the same thing, but it's functionally very close. Remember: Other scripts can still be appending to the same parent! It seems to work well in my isolated tests, but I don't know how well it works in the real world. For example: Could there be race conditions on high refresh rate monitors when `rAF` fires while the parser is still in the middle of processing a complex subtree of child elements?\n\n_Edit: In retrospect, it looks like I did test `html-parsed-element` back in 2023 and [past me claims](https://github.com/patricknelson/svelte-retag/issues/20#issuecomment-1848262955) that it didn't scale well:_\n\n> * Tried [`html-parsed-element`](https://github.com/WebReflection/html-parsed-element), however that results in high CPU usage by [traversing the DOM copiously](https://github.com/WebReflection/html-parsed-element/blob/v0.4.1/esm/index.js#L6-L12), checking for `nextSibling` on nodes elsewhere in the DOM. All just to _infer_ that the current tag has completed parsing. Just on [the demo site's IIFE page](https://svelte-retag.vercel.app/iife.html), profiled it taking ~500ms or more (iterating hundreds of times). This _does not_ scale well.\n ",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
